# Source builds every nomarchy host gets: <command>  <checkout>  <url>  <install command...>
#
# The fourth field is the REST of the line, so it may contain spaces. It runs with the
# checkout as the working directory.
#
# Why this file exists. shelf is Peter's own file manager and belongs on every nomarchy
# host, but it is not a package: there is no PKGBUILD and no AUR entry, so neither
# packages/repo.txt nor packages/aur.txt can express it. Before this file, nothing in the
# repo mentioned shelf at all -- and the failure that produced was precisely the kind this
# repo is built to prevent. On rigel 2026-09-20 `Mod+F` was dead because the binary was
# absent, while `umbriel validate` said `config: ok`, the keybind registered, and
# `bootstrap rigel` reported 25 ok / 0 to do / 0 conflicts. Every check passed and the key
# did nothing. A declared build turns that into one line of reported drift.
#
# The PROBE is the command name, looked for at ~/.local/bin/<command> and then on PATH.
# Both, deliberately: on a fresh host the bootstrap may run from a shell that predates the
# ~/.bash_profile symlink it is laying down, so ~/.local/bin is not necessarily on PATH yet,
# and a PATH-only probe would rebuild something already installed.
#
# UNLIKE the AUR list, the bootstrap will actually run these on --apply. The distinction is
# sudo, which is the script's one hard boundary: `makepkg -si` needs root to install the
# package it built, so the bootstrap can only print it, whereas these install user-local
# into ~/.local/bin and need no root at all. Dry run still shows the commands first.
#
# ORDERING: these need packages/repo.txt (gtk4) and bootstrap/toolchains (a Rust toolchain)
# already satisfied, which is why the bootstrap runs Packages, then Toolchains, then this.
#
# SSH remotes, matching how the nomarchy repo itself is cloned in docs/new-host/README.md
# phase 5 -- so a bootstrap already depends on the GitHub key being loaded, and these
# checkouts stay pushable rather than being read-only https copies of Peter's own work.

# shelf -- GTK4 file manager, bound to Mod+F in common/umbriel/base.toml, which matches its
# app_id ^dev\.shelf\.Shelf$. `make install` is user-local by design (PREFIX=$HOME/.local):
# it lays down ~/.local/bin/shelf, the desktop entry, and a user-level
# org.freedesktop.FileManager1 D-Bus service that takes precedence over a system one.
shelf	~/code/shelf	git@github.com:litescript/shelf.git	make install

# Irides -- palette designer; blueprints -> Noctalia palettes (common/irides/blueprints).
# `make install` is user-local (PREFIX=$HOME/.local): irides, irides-gtk, irides-lighting,
# the desktop entry, and ~/.local/share/irides/lighting.colors. Needs gtk4 and libadwaita
# >= 1.7 (packages/repo.txt) and a rustup toolchain: rust-toolchain.toml pins 1.98, which
# rustup fetches on first cargo run -- Arch's plain `rust` package would ignore the pin.
#
# NOT covered here, both per host and by hand for now:
#   - the Noctalia plugin behind Mod+Alt+T, which lives in Noctalia's own settings.toml and
#     needs Noctalia running -- a first-login step:
#       noctalia msg plugins source add irides path ~/code/irides/noctalia-plugins
#       noctalia msg plugins enable litescript/irides
#   - the lighting template link (~/.config/noctalia/irides-lighting.toml), only on hosts
#     with the RGB hardware, and only after this build (it runs irides-lighting).
irides	~/code/irides	git@github.com:litescript/irides.git	make install

